1. Primitive and Non-Primitive Data Types in Java

Primitive Data Types

Primitive data types are the basic built-in data types in Java. They store actual values directly in memory. Java has 8 primitive types

byte , 1 byte , byte b = 10; 
short, 2 bytes,  short s = 1000;
int,  4 bytes,  int x = 42; 
long,  8 bytes , long l = 100000L;
float,  4 bytes,  float f = 3.14f; 
double,  8 bytes,  double d = 3.14159; 
char,  2 bytes,  char c = 'A';
boolean , 1 bit,  boolean flag = true; 

Non-Primitive Data Types
Non-primitive data types store references to objects. 
Examples: String, arrays, classes, interfaces. 

int a = 10;
int b = a;   
b = 20;        

int[] arr1 = {1, 2, 3};
int[] arr2 = arr1;   
arr2[0] = 99;       


