3. JDK, JRE, and JVM

JVM (Java Virtual Machine)
The JVM is the engine that executes Java bytecode. It provides platform independence, memory management, garbage collection, and security. Each operating system has its own JVM implementation, but all JVMs understand the same bytecode.

JRE (Java Runtime Environment)
The JRE = It provides everything needed to run a Java application, but it cannot compile Java code.

JDK (Java Development Kit)
The JDK = It provides everything needed to write, compile, and run Java programs.

Role of Each Component
- JDK → used by developers to compile and build Java programs
- JRE → provides the environment and libraries to run Java programs
- JVM → actually executes the bytecode


4. JIT (Just-In-Time) Compiler

The JIT compiler is a part of the JVM's Execution Engine. It then compiles  bytecode into native machine code at runtime, so the CPU can execute it directly.

How JIT Improves Performance
1. Removes interpretation overhead
2. Applies optimizations
3. Caches compiled code


5. Relationship Between JDK, JRE, JVM, and JIT

The JDK contains the JRE, which contains the JVM, and the JIT compiler operates inside the JVM's execution engine. Together, the JDK compiles Java code, the JRE provides the runtime environment and libraries, the JVM executes the bytecode, and the JIT converts hot bytecode into fast native machine code for better performance.



